flags: --optimize=whole
mode: build

# **the merged-module half of the return ABI**, and the reason it is a `mode: build` case with
# `--optimize=whole` rather than an ordinary one: nothing else in the corpus builds a *binary* out of the
# merged module. `echoc run` merges too, so the plain-mode cases cover the JIT - but a JIT fault and a
# linked-binary fault are the same bug and only one of them is reproducible under a debugger.
#
# what broke here: an aggregate over Compiler::LLVM::return_abi_for's two-register line comes back through
# an `sret` pointer, and which *register* that pointer travels in is decided by the attribute on the
# **call**, not by the one on the function. LLVM hides a missing call-site attribute by reading the
# callee's - and `CallBase::getCalledFunction()` answers null when the call's FunctionType is not the
# callee's, which is precisely what a merge produces: `llvm::Linker` brings each unit's own named struct
# types along, so `string` ends up as both `%string` and `%string.3`, one shape under two identities. The
# attribute was dropped, every argument shifted one register, and a `string`'s size field arrived holding
# somebody else's pointer - a `memmove` of a garbage length, faulting nowhere near the cause.
#
# so this is an *output* case and not an IR one. The IR claim lives in strings/view_conversion, which pins
# the attribute at the call site; this pins that the program the two halves make actually runs.

--- OUT --->
3
a|b|c
[a-b-c]
